<template>
  <p>Building your own custom web app can feel daunting, especially if you are not a web developer. There are plenty of nice visual design tools out there, but you lose granular control if you do not write the code yourself. As someone who is not a web developer, I found Vue.js to be a great framework to start with: it is easy to learn and has thorough documentation and community support. In this series, I will show you how to build a chatbot using Vue.js and connect it to a back-end API that calls GPT-3.5. At the end, we will store the messages in a MongoDB database so that we can reuse them later.</p>
  <p>If you want to jump straight to the finished product, you can find the repository here.</p>
  <GithubRepoCard repo="lancewilhelm/vue-chatbot" />
  <p>In this part, we will work up to <a href="https://github.com/lancewilhelm/vue-chatbot/tree/62d4a06683ae7a49781eb8358a15a84406c34706" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">this commit.</a></p>
  <hr />
  <ProseHeading id="what-is-vuejs" :level="2">What is Vue.js?</ProseHeading>
  <p>Vue.js is a JavaScript framework for building user interfaces. It is a progressive framework, which means that you can start small and scale up as needed—especially helpful when you are learning front-end development. Vue.js is flexible enough to build single-page applications or add interactivity to existing webpages, and its lightweight footprint keeps web apps fast and responsive. You can read more about Vue.js <a href="https://vuejs.org/v2/guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">in their guide</a>.</p>
  <ProseHeading id="lets-get-started" :level="2">Let's get started</ProseHeading>
  <p>To get started, we need to install Node.js and npm. You can find the installation instructions <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a>. Once Node.js and npm are installed, create a new Vue.js project by running the following command in the directory where you want your project to live:</p>
  <CodeBlock language="bash" :code="code0" />
  <p>I named my project <code>app</code>, which will come up later in the series, though you can name yours whatever you want. Once the project is created, move into the project directory and start the development server:</p>
  <CodeBlock language="bash" :code="code1" />
  <p>Open the URL shown in the terminal, and you should see your first web app! Congrats—you are now a web developer! 🎉</p>
  <ProseHeading id="chatbot-bones" :level="2">Chatbot Bones</ProseHeading>
  <p>OK, maybe not a web developer yet, but you are on your way! Now that we have created the Vue.js app, let's look at its structure.</p>
  <BlogImage eager image-path="/img/vue-chatbot/vuejsstructure.png" alt="Vue project directory structure inside the app folder" caption="The basic Vue.js web app structure. All of the project files are located in the `app/` directory." width="300px" />
  <p>We do not need most of those starter files, so delete everything inside the <code>components/</code> directory. From here, we will build components for each piece of the app. That is one of the main advantages of frameworks like Vue.js and React: we can reuse the same component with different data in each instance, which will come in handy soon.</p>
  <p>Inside the <code>components/</code> directory, create our first component, <code>ChatContent.vue</code>, and place the following code in it.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="ChatContent.vue" :code="code2ChatContent" />
  <p>This file combines JavaScript, HTML, and CSS in one place. Later, this component will hold our chat messages, but for now it is just a purple box labeled "Chat Content". The <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block exports the component name, the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> block holds a basic <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, and the <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block styles that <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> by class name. Using the <code>scoped</code> attribute ensures that these styles apply only to this component, so we can reuse class names across components without style conflicts.</p>
  <p>Next, let's create a second component for the input row at the bottom of the chat window. Create a file called <code>InputRow.vue</code> and place the following code in it.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="InputRow.vue" :code="code2InputRow" />
  <p>This component looks much like <code>ChatContent.vue</code>, except that it renders a yellow box instead of a purple box. We will use it to hold the text input and send button.</p>
  <p>Your <code>components/</code> directory should now look like this:</p>
  <CodeBlock language="bash" :code="code3" />
  <p>Now we need to display both components on the screen. Back in <code>App.vue</code>, import and render them as shown below.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="App.vue" :code="code4" />
  <ProseHeading id="the-chatbubble-component" :level="2">The ChatBubble Component</ProseHeading>
  <p>Remember when I mentioned that reusable components would come in handy? This is why. We will create <code>ChatBubble.vue</code> to display individual chat messages inside <code>ChatContent.vue</code>. Create a file named <code>ChatBubble.vue</code> in your <code>components/</code> directory and add the following code.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="ChatBubble.vue" :code="code5" />
  <p>This renders a simple box with the text "Test" inside it. Next, open <code>ChatContent.vue</code>, import <code>ChatBubble.vue</code>, and use it to display five placeholder messages.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="ChatContent.vue" :code="code6" />
  <p>Notice the import statement at the top of <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, the <code>ChatBubble</code> entry inside <code>components</code>, and the five <code>&lt;ChatBubble /&gt;</code> tags in the template. This displays 5 placeholder chat bubbles in the chat area. Later, we will replace them with real chat messages.</p>
  <ProseHeading id="updating-the-inputrow-component" :level="2">Updating the InputRow Component</ProseHeading>
  <p>Let's add a text box and a button to <code>InputRow.vue</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="InputRow.vue" :code="code7" />
  <p>The chatbot is starting to look familiar now: we have a chat area and an input row with a text box and a send button. Your app should look something like this:</p>
  <BlogImage image-path="/img/vue-chatbot/chatbot1.png" alt="Initial chatbot interface" caption="Our app is starting to look like an actual chatbot app! But it has no real functionality yet..." width="300px" />
  <ProseHeading id="props-and-list-rendering" :level="2">Props and List Rendering</ProseHeading>
  <p>One of Vue.js's core features is passing data from a parent component to a child component through props. Let's update <code>ChatBubble.vue</code> to accept a prop called <code>message</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="ChatBubble.vue" :code="code8" />
  <p>We also style the bubble differently depending on whether it belongs to the user or the assistant. Each message object holds two key fields: the <code>role</code> (user or assistant) and the <code>content</code>. Those values are rendered inside the template using double curly braces (for example, <code>&#123;&#123; message.content &#125;&#125;</code>). Now let's update <code>ChatContent.vue</code> to pass the <code>message</code> prop to <code>ChatBubble.vue</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="html" filename="ChatContent.vue" :code="code9" />
  <p>Inside <code>data()</code>, we created a sample array of messages that will eventually be replaced by a prop from <code>App.vue</code>. For now, it gives us test data to work with. In the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>, we use the <code>v-for</code> directive to loop through the <code>messages</code> array and render a <code>ChatBubble</code> for each item, passing each message object through the <code>:message</code> prop. Your chatbot should now look something like this:</p>
  <BlogImage image-path="/img/vue-chatbot/chatbot2.png" alt="Chatbot interface with a message area and input" caption="Our chatbot is starting to look like a real chatbot! But it still has no real functionality..." width="300px" />
  <ProseHeading id="make-it-look-nice" :level="2">Make it look nice</ProseHeading>
  <p>Next, let's improve the styling. Right now the colors are harsh and everything is tiny. Let's update <code>main.css</code> in the <code>assets/</code> directory and refine the CSS in each component, including <code>App.vue</code>. Here is what the styles in those 5 files look like now.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="css" filename="main.css" :code="code10" />
  <CodeBlock language="css" filename="App.vue" :code="code11" />
  <CodeBlock language="css" filename="ChatContent.vue" :code="code12" />
  <CodeBlock language="css" filename="ChatBubble.vue" :code="code13" />
  <CodeBlock language="css" filename="InputRow.vue" :code="code14" />
  <ProseHeading id="now-we-are-looking-good" :level="2">Now we are looking good</ProseHeading>
  <p>Here is what our chatbot looks like now!</p>
  <BlogImage image-path="/img/vue-chatbot/chatbot3.png" alt="Styled chatbot interface showing multiple messages" caption="Some style makes it feel so much nicer. I added a ton of messages to test the scrolling ability." width="500px" />
  <ProseHeading id="thats-it-for-the-front-end-for-now" :level="2">That's it for the front-end for now</ProseHeading>
  <p>In the next part, we will build the FastAPI back-end and connect it to our front-end. In the final part, we will add GPT-3.5 and MongoDB.</p>
  <p>You can view all of the code at this stage at <a href="https://github.com/lancewilhelm/vue-chatbot/tree/62d4a06683ae7a49781eb8358a15a84406c34706" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">this GitHub commit</a>.</p>
